---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Param from "../../../components/Param.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="cli"
  title="The command line"
  description="The deptheos CLI for macOS, Windows, and Linux: stacking, batch processing, aligned frame export, grow and turntable sequences, redrawing a render with adjustments, benchmarks, and exit codes."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>The command line</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The desktop app comes with <code>deptheos</code>, a command that stacks from a terminal with the same
      engine, methods and export formats. It suits scripts, a folder watched by another tool, or a machine you
      reach over SSH. It lists the files it writes on standard output, one path per line, and its progress and
      errors on standard error.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="install">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Where it is</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>macOS</strong>: inside Deptheos.app. Choose Deptheos ▸ Install Command Line Tool… to link it
          into <code>/usr/local/bin</code>, which may ask for your password. Do it once the app is in
          Applications, not while it runs from the disk image. The link follows the app when it updates.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Windows</strong>: <code>deptheos.exe</code> beside the app, in the folder Deptheos is installed
          in. The installer's <em>Add the deptheos command to PATH, for the command line</em> option, on by default, lets a new
          terminal find it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Linux</strong>: the .deb and .rpm install it as <code>/usr/bin/deptheos</code>. In the
          tarball it is <code>bin/deptheos</code>; its <code>install.sh</code> copies it into
          <code>~/.local/bin</code>, or under <code>PREFIX</code> if you set one.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Its help lists every command, and <code>deptheos &lt;command&gt; --help</code> each command's options:</p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos --help</code></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="stack">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Stacking one bracket: pyramid, depth, wavelet</h2>
      <p>
        Each method is a command. Give it the frames, or their folder, and the result's file with
        <code>-o</code>; the extension picks the format (<code>.tif</code>, <code>.png</code>,
        <code>.jpg</code>, or <code>.dng</code> for camera RAW input).
      </p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos pyramid ~/shots/bee -o bee.tif</code></p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos depth IMG_*.CR3 -o bee.dng --radius 12</code></p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos wavelet ~/shots/bee -o bee.tif --denoise 2</code></p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="choose">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--smoothness</code></th><td><Param path="defaults.pyramid.smoothness" /></td><td>Pyramid's Smoothness, <Param path="ranges.pyramid.smoothness.min" /> to <Param path="ranges.pyramid.smoothness.max" />.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--radius</code>, <code>--smoothing</code>, <code>--blend-range</code></th><td><Param path="defaults.depth.radius" />, <Param path="defaults.depth.smoothing" />, <Param path="defaults.depth.blend_range" /></td><td>Depth's settings.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--denoise</code></th><td>1</td><td>Wavelet's Denoise, 0 to 10.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--consistency</code></th><td>0</td><td>Wavelet's consistency filter, 0 to 2 (0 off, 1 mild, 2 stronger).</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>-a</code>, <code>--align</code></th><td>ecc</td><td><code>ecc</code> (ECC: lines each frame up with the one before it), <code>grid</code> (Grid search: shift, rotation and magnification only), or <code>none</code> (no alignment, for frames already lined up).</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--no-crop</code></th><td></td><td>Keep the full frame instead of cropping to the area every frame covers.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--order</code></th><td>name</td><td><code>name</code>, or <code>time</code> for capture time.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--bit-depth</code></th><td>16 where the stack has 16</td><td>8 or 16, for TIFF and PNG.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--tiff-compression</code></th><td>zip</td><td><code>zip</code>, <code>lzw</code>, or <code>none</code>, for TIFF.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--jpeg-quality</code></th><td>92</td><td>1 to 100.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--raw-backend</code></th><td>libraw</td><td><code>libraw</code>, or <code>adobe-dng</code> to decode through Adobe DNG Converter.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--camera-profile</code></th><td>adobe</td><td>The Adobe camera profile that colors camera RAW decoded by LibRaw: <code>adobe</code>, <code>camera</code> or <code>none</code> (below).</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        With <code>--align grid</code>, set search limits with <code>--grid-shift-x</code> and
        <code>--grid-shift-y</code> (% of the frame's longer side), <code>--grid-rotation</code> (degrees),
        <code>--grid-magnification</code> (%), and the flag <code>--grid-brightness</code> (exposure
        compensation), matching the app's Settings:
      </p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos pyramid ~/shots/handheld -o handheld.tif --align grid --grid-shift-x 15 --grid-brightness</code></p>
      <p id="camera-profile">
        <code>--camera-profile</code> picks the installed <a href="/docs/raw/#settings">Adobe camera profile</a>
        that colors camera RAW frames decoded by LibRaw: <code>adobe</code> for Adobe Standard (the default),
        <code>camera</code> for Camera Standard, the camera maker's look, or <code>none</code> for the colors in
        the file, with no profile. When no profile for the camera is installed, LibRaw's own color matrices are
        used. <code>--camera-profile-folder</code> adds a folder of <code>.dcp</code> profiles to look in
        before Adobe's own, as the app's Camera profile folders setting does; give it once for each folder.
        Neither applies with <code>--raw-backend adobe-dng</code>, whose converted files carry their own
        profile, and DNG frames always use the profile stored in them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="batch">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>batch</h2>
      <p>
        Many folders at once, each folder one stack, written into the folder given to <code>-o</code> and named
        after its folder. An existing file is never replaced.
      </p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos batch pyramid ~/shots/* -o ~/stacked</code></p>
      <p>
        <code>--split auto</code> splits each folder into stacks by capture time, as the app's
        <a href="/docs/batch/#split">auto sort</a> does. <code>--format</code> chooses <code>tif</code> (the
        default), <code>png</code>, <code>jpg</code> or <code>dng</code>; with <code>dng</code>, stacks that
        aren't camera RAW are written as TIFF. <code>--source raw</code> or <code>standard</code> picks which
        file of a RAW + JPEG pair to stack.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="align">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>align</h2>
      <p>
        Aligns the frames without stacking them, and writes each one into the folder given to <code>-o</code>,
        named after its frame: <code>IMG_0001.CR3</code> becomes <code>IMG_0001.tif</code>. The frames are
        aligned exactly as a stack of them would be, with the same aligner, the same
        <a href="/docs/alignment/#settings">reverse check</a> and the same crop to the area every frame covers, so the
        files are all one size and line up pixel for pixel, ready to stack or retouch in another program. A
        frame that can't be aligned is left out, with a warning.
      </p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos align ~/shots/bee -o ~/bee-aligned</code></p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos align IMG_*.CR3 -o aligned --format dng</code></p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos align frames/ -o aligned --align grid --no-crop --format png</code></p>
      <p>
        It takes the stacking commands' input, alignment and RAW options: <code>--order</code>,
        <code>--align ecc</code> or <code>grid</code> with the grid's limits, <code>--no-crop</code>,
        <code>--raw-backend</code> and <code>--camera-profile</code>. <code>--format</code> chooses
        <code>tif</code> (the default), <code>png</code>, <code>jpg</code> or <code>dng</code>, for camera RAW
        frames only, with <code>--bit-depth</code>, <code>--jpeg-quality</code> and
        <code>--tiff-compression</code> as for a stack.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each file carries its color the way a stack's result in that format does: camera RAW rendered through
        the reference frame's color, or as a linear DNG with its color profile, and JPEG, PNG and TIFF frames in
        their own color space, with its profile embedded. Each keeps its own frame's camera, lens, exposure and
        capture time. Nothing is replaced: if a file it would write is already in the folder, it stops before
        reading any frame. The frames are read twice, once to align them all and once to write each, and only
        one aligned frame is held in memory at a time.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="sequence">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>sequence grow, sequence turntable</h2>
      <p>
        Exports numbered frames for a video editor into a new folder under <code>-o</code>, with a
        <code>manifest.json</code>: <code>grow</code> renders a progressive focus-sweep reveal as frames are
        added to the stack, and <code>turntable</code> stacks one bracket per angle for a rotating 360° view.
      </p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos sequence grow ~/shots/bee -o ~/sequences</code></p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos sequence turntable ~/turn/s* -o ~/sequences --mode both</code></p>
      <p>
        <code>grow</code> takes a bracket's frames and builds
        from the back unless you add <code>--front-to-back</code>; <code>--every 2</code> writes every second
        frame. <code>turntable</code> takes one folder per angle; <code>--mode</code> is <code>grow</code> (the
        default), <code>full</code> or <code>both</code>. <code>--long-edge</code> scales the frames down.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="redraw">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>redraw</h2>
      <p>
        Draws a render saved in the Library again, with its retouching and the adjustments given as flags, and
        writes it to <code>-o</code>. It reads only that render's folder (<code>Renders/</code> and the
        render's id, in the <a href="/docs/install/#data">Library folder</a>), never changes it, and runs while
        the app is open. It draws the flags' values, not the sliders the render has in
        <a href="/docs/adjust/">Adjust</a>; a slider not given stays where it starts.
      </p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos redraw ~/Library/Application\ Support/Deptheos/Renders/&lt;id&gt; -o bee.tif --exposure 0.5 --shadows 30 --clarity 15</code></p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos redraw ~/Library/Application\ Support/Deptheos/Renders/&lt;id&gt; -o bee.dng --temperature 5200 --reframe 400,300,6000,4000 --turn 90</code></p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="choose">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">Range</th><th scope="col">What it sets</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--exposure</code></th><td><Param path="adjust.exposure.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.exposure.max" /></td><td>Exposure, in stops.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--temperature</code>, <code>--tint</code></th><td>RAW: <Param path="adjust.temperature_kelvin.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.temperature_kelvin.max" /> K, tint <Param path="adjust.tint_raw.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.tint_raw.max" />. Others: <Param path="adjust.temperature_relative.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.temperature_relative.max" /></td><td>White balance; as shot when not given.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--contrast</code>, <code>--highlights</code>, <code>--shadows</code>, <code>--whites</code>, <code>--blacks</code></th><td><Param path="adjust.contrast.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.contrast.max" /></td><td>The Tone sliders.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--clarity</code>, <code>--texture</code>, <code>--vibrance</code>, <code>--saturation</code></th><td><Param path="adjust.clarity.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.clarity.max" /></td><td>The Presence sliders.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--sharpening</code></th><td><Param path="adjust.sharpening.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.sharpening.max" /></td><td>Sharpening's Amount.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--sharpen-radius</code>, <code>--sharpen-detail</code>, <code>--sharpen-masking</code></th><td><Param path="adjust.sharpen_radius.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.sharpen_radius.max" /> px; <Param path="adjust.sharpen_detail.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.sharpen_detail.max" /></td><td>Its Radius (from <Param path="adjust.sharpen_radius.default" />), Detail (from <Param path="adjust.sharpen_detail.default" />) and Masking (from <Param path="adjust.sharpen_masking.default" />).</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--vignette</code>, <code>--vignette-midpoint</code></th><td><Param path="adjust.vignette.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.vignette.max" />; <Param path="adjust.vignette_midpoint.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.vignette_midpoint.max" /></td><td>The vignette, centered on <code>--reframe</code>, and where it starts (from <Param path="adjust.vignette_midpoint.default" />).</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--reframe</code></th><td><code>X,Y,W,H</code></td><td>Adjust's Crop: the rectangle kept, in the stored picture's pixels. Not stacking's <code>--no-crop</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--turn</code>, <code>--flip</code></th><td><code>90</code>, <code>180</code>, <code>270</code>; <code>horizontal</code>, <code>vertical</code></td><td>Turn clockwise, after any flip; mirror.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--space</code></th><td><code>srgb</code>, <code>own</code>, <code>display-p3</code>, <code>adobe-rgb</code>, <code>prophoto</code></td><td>The color space written: <code>own</code> (the frames') for standard images, <code>srgb</code> for camera RAW at first. The wide spaces are for camera RAW; <code>prophoto</code> writes 16 bits only.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>--process</code></th><td>a number</td><td>The process version the values are read in, the latest at first.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The extension of <code>-o</code> picks the format, as for a stack, with <code>--bit-depth</code>,
        <code>--jpeg-quality</code> and <code>--tiff-compression</code>. A <code>.dng</code>, from a camera RAW
        render only, keeps every pixel and takes only the white balance, <code>--reframe</code>,
        <code>--turn</code> and <code>--flip</code>, as settings a RAW editor reads; it refuses the other
        sliders and <code>--space</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="bench">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>bench</h2>
      <p>
        Times the methods and aligners on your own frames, whole stacks each (Pyramid and Depth, each with every aligner, unless <code>--stacker</code> and <code>--align</code> choose), and prints a Markdown table of the times. Add <code>--live</code> to stack as the app does, with its live preview.
        <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/#timings">Timings</a> says more.
      </p>
      <p><code class="cmd">deptheos bench ~/shots/bee --live --stacker pyramid --align ecc --repeat 5 --cool 30</code></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="exit">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Exit status</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="choose">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Code</th><th scope="col">Means</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">0</th><td>Everything was made and written.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>An error stopped the run, or every stack of a turntable failed.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>The command line was refused, or a batch or turntable finished with one or more stacks that failed or could not be written.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">130</th><td>Interrupted (Ctrl-C, on macOS and Linux); what was written is kept.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p><code>-q</code> prints only warnings and errors; <code>-v</code> adds the engine's steps.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
